Z||000002||DRH||10-31-2001||11:53 PM||||Too much Tylenol can cause permanent liver damage. I'm not sure how much it takes, if it has to be all at once or if it's cumulative. I'd think a whole bottle would be bad news (especially if it's the economy size). You might want to talk to a doc about 9 at a time before you do it too many times...||||reg||1
Z||000003||frontal lobe||11-01-2001||12:08 AM||||DO NOT take a large amount of tylenol AT ONCE.  You will overwhelm the main system of metabolizing the tylenol, and your liver will kick into it's secondary metabolism pathway for tylenol.  Only problem with that is that the secondary metabolite is toxic to the liver.  So you can PERMANENTLY trash your liver and die a slow and painful death over several days.  Tylenol damage on the liver is generally NOT a cumulative thing-but if you are taking 9 at a time on a somewhat regular basis, YOU ARE PUSHING IT, and asking for permanent liver problems.<P>Taking a bottle of aspirin all at once, however, causes severe acidosis in the body and can kill you within hours to a day.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||11-01-2001||12:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hippocrat:<BR><B>Hard to say. <P>I Take 8 or 9 x strength tylenol for a headache. can't imagine a bottle would do that much damage. Unless of course its like T3s or something. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>PLEASE TALK TO YOUR DOC BEFOR DOING THAT AGAIN codine is safer may he can write a script for you but that much can kill<BR>too much of any thing can be bad<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||11-01-2001||01:26 AM||||I go through an economy size bottle of extra strength asprin (is that spelled right?) every week for my back.  No nasty side effect yet and I've been doing that for better than a year.  Not saying that it's a good thing, just that it hasn't affected me yet.  BTW--Theas98a ateas no asiiiide thesaiod;afasd efffttects to thiasdsf oi this whatsoeverrrrrrrrrrr. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||11-01-2001||09:37 AM||||I take 5 Advil in the morning, 5 in the afternoon and 5 at night.  I get really nasty headaches due to my neck and back...only way I can get through a day.<P>My blood is so thin, that if you poke me, I literally bleed without clotting.  <P>Ouch!  Dang pin pricks!  Well would you look at that, I sprung a leak!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Kinda looks like a water hose under pressure with a hole and water spraying out from it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyways...<P>I went to 3 Advils and 2 Xtra Strength Tylenols for awhile and per my Chiropractors recommendation as all my muscles in my back tightened up.  Tylenols do not agree with me.  I get very sick from em...so I quit.<P>Now if you will excuse me, I have to go roll up a joint for medicinal purposes.  JUST KIDDING!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||baptistheart||11-01-2001||09:50 AM||||Anyone that has really bad headaches two or more times a week in my opinion should go get their blood pressure and thyroid checked.<BR> I used to have horrible headaches three or four times a week. I thought it was sinus and my chiropractor told me it was my neck.  <P><BR> It turned out i had high blood pressure and a very low thyroid and as soon as i started the meds it all went away.<P> Havent had a headache in over 6 months.<P> Just Thought i'd run it past ya.<P>------------------<BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||11-01-2001||09:51 AM||||Aspirin contains a substance that will cause your blood not to cloth, which is a very dangerous situation. If you would take a bottle of aspirin, you'd probably get a stroke within a few hours.<P>High dosages of Tylenol will cause liver failure.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||11-01-2001||10:04 AM||||High doses of Tylenol can cause death.... immediately!  For some reason, the makers of Tylenol do not want to prominantly display that on the box but in high enough doses it is fatal.  Continued use could cause liver damage/failure but if you eat enough at once there won't be any prolonged, painful death.  Your going to be dead on the spot.<P>Advil can cause ulcers and other stomach damage in high doses.  Ever get prescription ibuprofin?  The bottle says to take it with lots of food!  So if you are taking a lot of it be sure to eat with it!  <P>Basically, if your having big enough problems that you feel the need to eat a lot of over the counter pain killers like these, and haven't been checked out by the doc, go get checked out by him!  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||frontal lobe||11-01-2001||10:13 AM||||It only takes 325mg of aspirin to irreversibly inhibit ALL of your platelets.  Certainly no more than 500mg.  So if you take 1-2 aspirin, or 10 or 100 aspirin, your blood "thinning" won't be any different.<P>As you take A LOT of aspirin ALL AT ONCE, you can overwhelm your systems ability to buffer ACID, and make yourself quite ill.  <P>If you take 5-6 at a time 3-4 times a day on a regular basis, your blood won't be thinner and you won't overwhelm your buffering system, but you WILL increase your risk of ulcer and WILL increase your risk of kidney damage.<P>P.S.  Don't you get ringing in the ears when you take that much aspirin??<P>Tylenol DOES NOT cause immediate death.  Overdoses cause production of a toxic metabolite that will destroy your liver within a day, so that you are as good as dead, but the actual death is a painful process that usually lasts several days.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||11-01-2001||01:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>If you take 5-6 at a time 3-4 times a day on a regular basis, your blood won't be thinner and you won't overwhelm your buffering system, but you WILL increase your risk of ulcer and WILL increase your risk of kidney damage.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I used to take 2-3 Extra Strength Bufferin for neck pain. Sometimes several times a day. Sometimes none at all.<BR>Ended up with a bleeding ulcer <I>that I couldn't feel</I>. Blood count was down to 9. I understand they transfuse at 8. Spent December 20-23 in the hospital. Not fun. <P>Also, if you like to drink a bit of alcohol at night, DON'T take Tylenol before going to bed to "prevent" a hangover. As I understand it, your body will metabolize the alcohol first, and the Tylenol will "sit there" with toxic effects.<BR>(Doc? Care to expand on or correct this?)<P>Be careful with meds. <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DaRkLoRD||11-01-2001||01:34 PM||||I had to get a friend to the hospital because he OD'd on tylenol this summer.. he's ok now, but he was pretty sick by the time he arrived at the ER.<P>I've been having a lot of really bad headaches lately, and as a result have been taking quite a bit of robaxacet (acetominiphen plus a muscle relaxant, helps loosen up my neck muscles..) but.. I want to find out exactly what's causing the headaches. my family doctor sent me for a CT scan, which came back fine.. so he put me on medicine to prevent headache symptoms. but I want to find and eliminate/treat the cause... I think I gotta find a new doctor.. one that's not so quick to dispense pills for everything I have, without finding the cause.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000013||JSocha||11-01-2001||01:54 PM||||Overall, my BP and thyroid are fine.<P>Dad's bad genetics that he passed onto us.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Both my older brothers, older sister, me and my younger sister all have about the same neck and back problems and in the same area as our father.<P>Unfortunately, I've now got it more severe then they do.  When I was in 6th grade, I took up wrestling and the coach did a head lock on me and somehow twisted/kinked my neck really bad.  My neck hurt for months after that.  <P>In 8th grade, I injured my left knee at Karate practice, was on crutches, had surgery, blah...blah...blah...blah, and ever since I have had a trick knee, so I favor one side of my body more then the other and have rotated my back over time when my left knee goes out and I start to favor that side.<P>So, now my neck locks up at the base of my skull a lot worse then it does the rest of my family and I get nasty, nasty headaches from the tension that is created either during the day or while I sleep.<P>Somedays it is so bad, I get sick, can't even get out of bed or sleep because it feels like my head is being pulled apart from both sides.<P>Asprins just help me make it through the day or until my neck/back relax.<P>After a Chiropractor adjustment, my headache typically peak for roughly an hour (or the drive home...wife driving) then goes away and I'm fine for several days or until my neck/back starts going out again.  <P>The last time I went just recently on a Sunday, the Chiropractor had to prescribed me some muscle relaxants just because my muscles were pulling every which way from Sunday.<P>Also, doesn't help looking at a computer monitor all day either and constantly thinking.  Thinking hurts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||11-01-2001||02:06 PM||||Steve, Jeff,<P>Drink a lot of caffeine?<BR>Been in any car wrecks?<BR>My neck bothers me on a regular basis. X-rays show some arthritic looking stuff. <P>Doctor told me to get a "cervical traction device". It's a sling that goes around your head and chin, with a cord that is fed up through some pulleys that are hooked to the top of a door, counter-weighted with a bag filled with water. (7 lbs., in my case.)<BR>It's designed to take all the weight of your head off of your neck muscles.<BR>When my neck bothers me, I use it for 15-20 minutes. Does wonders! <BR>You can buy it without a prescription. Mine was like $35 US. <P>Caffeine also knots up my neck muscles. Anything more than 2-3 Diet Cokes a day is enough to bother me. <BR>If you drink a lot of coffee, or take caffeine or "ripped fuel" pills, that could be a contributor.<P>Just my $.02.<P>Raydar<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||JSocha||11-01-2001||02:17 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Drink a lot of caffeine?<BR>Been in any car wrecks?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>2 - 2 liter bottles of Diet Pepsi a day. <P>Two car accidents (1 roll over...1 rear ending the tire of a tractor)...however...had my neck/back problems way, way before that and/or the other injuries.<P>Was diagnosed once with severe eyestrain as the cause.  That optomitrist doesn't practice anymore.  Found out that I never had eyestrain when the problem still continued.<P>I never believed in Chiropractors.  I thought they were just a bunch of quacks.  <P>Now I will swear by them and you won't be able to convince me otherwise.  Matter of fact, my Chiropractor is so good...he can move the pain from my neck and/or back right into my wallet.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000016||frontal lobe||11-01-2001||02:50 PM||||Raydar, WE WANT TO SEE PICS of you in your device!<P>Kidding.  Sorry about the ulcer.  Bummer.  Hgb below 8, you are definitely thinking about transfusion.<P>DaRkLoRD, headaches are very difficult to diagnose as to the exact cause, as the ONLY tests available that MIGHT give an answer are CT scans or MRI scans.  At least your doctor had you have that.  Otherwise, you just try to figure out the cause from the history, which is why Raydar was asking all those questions.  If you do go see another doctor, that is what they should be doing and not more tests, because there really aren't any more.  Sorry.<P>Regarding the tylenol, ibuprofen, or aspirin use, look at it in terms of car engines.  Your engine should have no reason it can't tolerate 4,000 rpm shifts all day, week, month, and year long.  There shouldn't really be any cumulative damage from doing that, because it is well within the tolerances of the engine.  However, with a Fiero engine, you probably won't get many more than one trip up in the 8,000 rpm range.  Then the damage is permanent and irreparable.  Because you have exceeded the limit of what it can handle.  That is what happens with overdoses of tylenol, aspirin, or ibuprofen.  The prescribed or recommended doses would be fine for most people (sorry, not you Raydar) for LONG periods of time-even life-long.  Because they don't exceed the limit.  But overdose and exceed the limit and you can't handle that often even more than once.  Yes, there are people like Raydar that have problems even at prescribed doses, just as there are engines that can break down even when used at prescribed rpm's.  But it usually doesn't happen.<P>Matthew_Fiero, you started the thread.  Do you still have questions, or does that cover it?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||11-01-2001||03:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>Raydar, WE WANT TO SEE PICS of you in your device!<P>Kidding.  Sorry about the ulcer.  Bummer.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not gunna happen.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Regarding the ulcer... I tested positive for <I>h.pylori.</I> Did two weeks of Helidac therapy (antibiotics and bismuth tabs) for it. Haven't had any trouble, since. <BR>Still haven't taken any aspirin, but I can eat all the Mexican food that I want.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>For those that don't know... <I>h.pylori</I> is a bacteria that has been identified as making certain persons (not everyone) susceptible to ulcers. <BR>Comes from contaminated food or water. If you've got ulcers, it's definitely worth getting tested for it, IMHO.<P>Sorry for dragging this off-topic. I just thought this info might be of benefit. <P>As you were. <P>raydar||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cliff Pennock||11-01-2001||04:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>2 - 2 liter bottles of Diet Pepsi a day.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Diet Pepsi contains Aspartame which causes severe headaches and migraine attacks for a lot of people. Me for instance. I used to have one or two severe migraine attacks <B>per week</B> for years at end. I drank about 1 liter of Diet Coke per day. Since the day I stopped drinking/eating anything that contains Aspartame this has been reduced to about once/twice a year... And those few migraine attacks are usually because I accidently drink or eat something with Aspertame (like chewing gum).||||reg||1
Z||000019||frontal lobe||11-01-2001||05:16 PM||||Very true, Cliff.  So what are you drinking instead??  I don't want the calories from fruit juices (I like to chew my calories, not drink them) and the water around me is terrible.<P>Also, MSG (monosodium glutamate) gives a lot of people headaches.  So do nitrates in many processed sandwich meats.<P>Some people give major headaches, too.  Treatment is a "ban-ectomy".||||reg||1
Z||000020||DaRkLoRD||11-01-2001||05:51 PM||||I try to avoid drinks with caffiene, though I do like iced tea... also, I've been in a couple of very minor accidents, no injuries resulted from either one.<P>My headaches often come very suddenly, I get a bright flash of light in my field of vision, often it darts from one point off to another area outside my field of view.. other times I get an expanding blind spot, and I can't see anything within it.. other times I cannot control the focus of my eyes, and everything is blurry no matter how hard I try to focus. All 3 of these situations is accompanied by an intense feeling of nausea followed by a dull throbbing pain which lasts for hours, or about 45 mins if I take a couple of robaxacets with a glass of water and get a damp cloth to put over my eyes.<BR>I get about 3-5 of these headaches a week, and often have to stop working and put my hands over my eyes to block out all light, because my vision starts to flicker at times.. (if I don't do this, then it progresses to a full-fledged headache with one or several of the symptoms I mentioned above)<P>The CT scan came back negative, there's nothing physically wrong with my brain.. they thought it could have been a tumor for a while, but thankfully it's not. However.. instead of continuing to try and find the problem, my doctor just put me on stuff to control headache symptoms.. and then more stuff to deal with the side effects of this medication (affects your blood pressure among other things)<P>any ideas what might be causing these headaches? no change in diet, nothing else has changed except for my job.. went from freelance contract work to full time contract work, in an office with flickery lights (that the idiot maintenance people will not change..... they're always too busy drinking coffee to do any real work.. we put in a work request 2 months ago and the bulbs have not been changed yet. and union rules won't let us change it ourselves)<P>One of the best treatments I've found for headaches and joint pain (esp. neck muscles) is floating in a warm pool... I wish I had a float tank in my house, tremendous benefits for relaxation, and it only takes up about as much space as a small 2-seat sofa.<BR>as for a chiropractor.. I prefer a good massage to getting my back cracked and neck snapped around.. but they both seem to work well.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>     <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000021||JSocha||11-01-2001||06:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>Some people give major headaches, too.  Treatment is a "ban-ectomy".</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B>ROFLMAO!</B>  Thanks!  I needed that laugh today as today has been a loooooonnnnnngggggg day!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||14
Z||000022||frontal lobe||11-01-2001||06:34 PM||||DaRkLoRD,<P>Well there is NO mystery at all what is causing your type of headaches.  These are absolutely textbook, classic true MIGRAINE headaches.  I think a good junior medical student could have diagnosed that one.<P>There is no STRUCTURAL problems with your body, so no testing is going to reveal anything, nor should it.  Your problem is FUNCTIONAL-a body part malfunctioning temporarily.<P>No one knows WHAT causes migraines.  So you should really change your focus of trying to find out what is causing your headaches.  Any further search will be futile, unfortunately. But what happens is you get a spasm (cramp) in one of the arteries in your brain-in your case in the occipital lobe because that is where vision input is decoded and transformed into images.  That part of your brain starts to get inadequate blood flow due to the spasm.  Then that part of the brain can't function as well, so can't process the visual input accurately and correctly.  You've had other similar episodes when you hang the arm over the edge of a chair and your arm starts to tingle-the blood supply is getting cut off to the nerve in your arm and so the nerve malfunctions temporarily and you get the tingling.  Well, in the occipital part of the brain, you don't get a tingling because that isn't its department, but it's equivalent is the funny vision sensations you have.<P>Your brain doesn't get enough oxygen to make energy for itself, so the brain switches to anaerobic metabolism, which isn't nearly as efficient but it's better than nothing.  The by-product of anaerobic metabolism is lactic acid.  Lactic acid is an arterial vasodilator-so the lactic acid opens up the spasmed artery.  That's the safety mechanism to not let you have a stroke from this.  However, now the brain is irritated from not having enough blood, the artery is dilated and all this blood can now come pounding in, and you get a headache.  <P>So there you go.  That's the long but complete story.  However, there are different triggers for migraines for different people.  And there are no treatments for migraines for all people that are say 95% effective.  So it gets frustrating trying different medicines for people until you hit on the right combination of effectiveness and no-to-minimal side effects.  But you better get a skilled doctor who you can communicate with and work with, because that is what it takes to find a regimen that controls your headaches and works for you.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Cliff Pennock||11-01-2001||06:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>My headaches often come very suddenly, I get a bright flash of light in my field of vision, often it darts from one point off to another area outside my field of view.. other times I get an expanding blind spot, and I can't see anything within it.. other times I cannot control the focus of my eyes, and everything is blurry no matter how hard I try to focus.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There's your typical migraine attack. Like I said, avoid <B>everything</B> with Aspartame in it (it's always listed as one of the ingredients). For the nausea you can take <B>Metoclopramide</B> (or Primperan as it is known in Europe), for the pain <B>Paracetamol</B> and to get rid of the migraine <B>Sumatriptan</B> (or Imigran in Europe).<P>Now the Sumatriptan will set off a small bomb in your body (loss of energy, hot flashes) but everything's better than the migraine attack itself. It should get rid of the attack within 15 minutes.<P>Migraine is very common, yet medical science still has no idea what it is or what causes it. Like I said, avoiding Aspartame and Mono Sodium Glutaminate (also known as Ve-tsin, a "spice" used in many oriental recipes) has lowered the number of attacks from weekly to bi-yearly for me.<P>The trouble with Aspartame is that it is addicting. That's why most people who drink diet drinks, drink <B>a lot</B> of it. Breaking the addiction is just as hard as to stop smoking, for instance. I now drink lots of water and coffee (the later isn't really a good alternative) and occasionally an energy drink. Ice-tea is a very good alternative.||||reg||1
Z||000024||DaRkLoRD||11-01-2001||06:55 PM||||I've known for years that they're migraine headaches, as has my doctor.. what I meant was they're happening much more frequently now, and I don't know why. they used to only happen 1-2 times a year, now they're 3-5 times a week, sometimes 2 in 1 day.<P>I never drink diet cola, nor do I use artificial sweetner in my drinks. when I drink coffee (cafe mocha actually, can't stand straight coffee) I always use real sugar.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
