Z||000055||frontal lobe||11-05-2001||01:23 AM||||I didn't see the original post, JohnnyK.<P>Spewing generally has a slightly negative connotation that goes with it, kind of like "indiscriminate hurling around".  If I was doing that I wasn't meaning to.  These are some topics I have a lot of experience with, having gone to pharmacy school, then medical school, then residency training, then 13 years of working now.<P>I was just trying to be as accurate and helpful as possible.  Did it come across differently?<P>Anyway, I'm pretty thick skinned so I would have just shrugged off the spew remark.  But Hugh, I do appreciate you sticking up for in that you didn't think I was being negative in the thread in any way.<P>Fortunately I don't have any first-hand experience with decapitation!!<P>Most of my experience in this area is with patients having arrhythmias with pauses.  In the U.S., medicare will pay for people with pauses in heartbeats of &gt;3 seconds, because of the risk/probability of fainting.  That was my basis for the approximate 4 seconds to pass out.  So there is no reason if someone gets the guillotine that they wouldn't be conscious for that amount of time-time to blink, see the inside of the basket, before passing out.  Nice.||||reg||1
