Z||000059||MsBeth||11-07-2001||02:29 AM||||Darklord~<P>Migraine triggers vary person to person. <P>I have suffered for over 30 years (the worst time was about 15 years ago I'd get them for at least a week at a time, they would go away for a day or 2 then come back for another week) I was so sick I weighed about 87lbs and couldn't eat or drink anything...it was horrible.  As the years pasted they ebbed a bit and a few years ago they started reappearing ~ I went for a CAT scan and saw a neurologist.   I have a 5 step plan to battle my migraines ...they include Aspirin, Reglan (Brand name in the states ~ the generic name is Metoclopramide for the nausea) Zomig ( generic name zolmitriptan) and my last line of defense is Maxalt (don't remember the generic name it is a newer product- but very effective).  (Btw..Sumatriptan is known as Imitrex in the states and is available as a pill, an injection, or the new nasal spray)<P>One of the most important things in treating a migraine headache is that once you become "sick to your stomach" don't take anything until you are no longer feeling sick... the chemicals that are produced during nausea inhibits the bodies ability to properly absorb the medications used to battle the migraine. <P>Only your doctor can properly advise you on which medication is the right one for you.  I searched for 30 years before I found the right ones for me ( I've tried them all ... OTC - over the counters, beta blockers, blood pressure meds, and the entire line up of migraine meds, Chiropractors, Physical Therapists, meditation, aromatherapy, herbal remedies- everything).<P>Keep in mind that narcotics (aka codeine and the like) will actually cause a headache.  As will taking too many aspirin or ibuprofen or acetaminophen, those are called boomerang headaches... <P>Find a good neurologist... and good luck.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blue_2.gif">  <P>MsBeth||||reg||1
Z||000060||DaRkLoRD||11-07-2001||08:51 AM||||My doctor loves giving out pills. When my CT scan results came back showing everything fine, my doctr basically said "ok, they're migraines. here's a bunch of painkillers" and other stuff. nevermind continuing to find the problem, that wouldn't happen in the usa I bet.<P>anyway, I might have to go find a neurologist on my own, my family doctor probably won't be much help.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000061||frontal lobe||11-07-2001||10:07 AM||||DaRkLoRD, sad to say it actually often would happen in the U.S.  LOTS of doctors are intellectually lazy and won't take the time to do the investigative work with a patient to find out what their migraine triggers are.  <P>All you need to do, though, is find ONE good one who will work with you.  When you do, it will change your life.  Really.||||reg||1
